sysl.regex — running the program
================================

    module sysl.regex

    import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
    import sysl.text.{Chars, chars_of}

Thompson's 1968 construction, extended by Rob Pike to carry capture positions. Every branch of the
pattern is followed at once rather than one at a time with backtracking, so the cost is the input
length times the program length and there is no pattern that makes it explode. `(a|a)*b` against
thirty `a`s and no `b` is the standard demonstration: a backtracking engine takes a billion steps,
and this takes about nine hundred.

The whole of that guarantee rests on one thing — **at most one thread per program counter per
step**. Two threads at the same instruction with the same input position will do exactly the same
thing from here on, so the second is redundant, and dropping it is what bounds the thread list by
the program length instead of by the number of paths.


How a step is bounded
---------------------

The redundancy check is a `marks` array, one entry per instruction, holding the step at which that
instruction was last added. It is one array for the whole run rather than one per thread, and that
is the correction that matters most against the engine this is modelled on: there, a fresh array was
allocated inside the character loop *for each thread*, so the check only ever deduplicated within
one thread's own epsilon closure and the list could hold the same program counter many times over.

Stamping the step number rather than clearing a flag array is what makes one array enough. The list
being built for the *next* position is stamped with the next step, and when that list becomes the
current one its stamps are already the ones the new step will compare against — so the thread
started at the new position is deduplicated against the threads that arrived there from the previous
one, which two separate arrays could not have managed.

    private struct Thread
        pc: usize
        saves: []int
    end Thread

A capture slot holds a byte offset, or `-1` for a group that has not matched. The engine this comes
from wrote a `-2` sentinel at save time and then swept **every slot of every thread at every input
position** to replace the sentinels with the real position; the position is known when the `Save`
runs, so it is simply written, and the sweep is gone.

    private const unset: int = -1

    private struct Vm
        prog: Program
        src: []const u8
        slots: usize
        marks: []int

The epsilon closure. Everything that does not consume a character is followed here and now, so what
lands in the list is only ever an instruction that looks at the input — which is what keeps the
character loop to one `match` over three cases and an accept.

`Save` is the one case that copies. A thread's slots are shared with whatever it was forked from
until it writes one, at which point it needs its own; copying at that moment is what makes the fork
in a `Split` free. It is the remaining cost of the simple representation — a persistent structure
would share the unchanged slots too — and it is bounded by the number of `Save` instructions the
pattern has rather than by the input length.

        add(*self, list: &Buf[Thread], pc: usize, saves: []int, gen: int, at: usize)
            if pc >= self.prog.len() then return
            if self.marks[pc] == gen then return

            self.marks[pc] = gen

            self.prog.at(pc) match
                Jump(to) ->
                    self.add(list, to, saves, gen, at)
                Split(a, b) ->
                    self.add(list, a, saves, gen, at)
                    self.add(list, b, saves, gen, at)
                Save(slot) ->
                    var next: []int = [unset; self.slots]

                    for i in 0usize..<self.slots
                        next[i] = saves[i]

                    next[slot] = int(at)
                    self.add(list, pc + 1usize, next, gen, at)
                AssertStart ->
                    if at == 0usize then self.add(list, pc + 1usize, saves, gen, at)
                AssertEnd ->
                    if at == self.src.len then self.add(list, pc + 1usize, saves, gen, at)
                else
                    list.push(Thread(pc, saves))
        end add
    end Vm


Leftmost, then longest
----------------------

POSIX asks for the match that begins earliest, and among those the one that runs longest. Both fall
out of how the run is arranged rather than from a search over candidates:

  * **Leftmost** — a thread is started at every input position, but only until something matches.
    After that no new starting position is admitted, so nothing that begins later can win.
  * **Longest** — the run does not stop at the first accept. It keeps going while any thread is
    alive, and each accept replaces the best so far when it ends later at the same start.

**What is not implemented is POSIX's rule for the subexpressions.** The whole match is leftmost and
longest; a capture group inside it holds what the preferred path through the pattern gave it, which
is the greedy reading rather than the one POSIX derives for each group in turn. That is the same
choice Go and Rust make, and it is written down here rather than claimed away because the two differ
on patterns like `(a|ab)(c|bcd)` — worth knowing before relying on a group.

    exec(prog: Program, input: string) -> Option[[]int] = exec_from(prog, input, 0usize)

A run may be told to begin part way in, which is what a caller walking every match in a text needs.
It is a *starting position*, not a smaller input: `^` still means the beginning of the whole text and
`$` its end, so the second match in a line is not handed a `^` that suddenly holds. Slicing the input
and searching the remainder — the obvious way to write `find_all` — gets exactly that wrong.

    exec_from(prog: Program, input: string, from: usize) -> Option[[]int]
        var src = input.bytes
        var slots = prog.slots()
        var vm = Vm(prog, src, slots, [unset; prog.len()])

        for i in 0usize..<prog.len()
            vm.marks[i] = -1

        var clist: &Buf[Thread] = buf()
        var nlist: &Buf[Thread] = buf()
        var best: []int = [unset; slots]
        var matched = false
        var cur = Chars(src, from)
        var gen = 0
        var going = true

        while going
            var at = cur.offset
            var here = cur.peek()
            var look = cur

            look.next()

            var after = look.offset

            if !matched
                vm.add(clist, 0usize, [unset; slots], gen, at)

            nlist.clear()

            for i in 0usize..<clist.len()
                var t = clist.at(i)

                vm.prog.at(t.pc) match
                    MatchChar(want) ->
                        if here.is_some() && here.unwrap() == want then
                            vm.add(nlist, t.pc + 1usize, t.saves, gen + 1, after)
                    MatchAny ->
                        if here.is_some() then
                            vm.add(nlist, t.pc + 1usize, t.saves, gen + 1, after)
                    MatchSet(cls) ->
                        if here.is_some() && cls.admits(here.unwrap()) then
                            vm.add(nlist, t.pc + 1usize, t.saves, gen + 1, after)
                    Accept ->
                        if !matched || t.saves[0] < best[0] ||
                                (t.saves[0] == best[0] && t.saves[1] > best[1])
                            matched = true

                            for s in 0usize..<slots
                                best[s] = t.saves[s]
                    else
                        print("panic: an epsilon instruction reached the thread list")
                        exit(1)

            var swap = clist

            clist = nlist
            nlist = swap
            gen += 1

            if here.is_none() then going = false
            elif clist.len() == 0usize && matched then going = false
            else cur.next()

        if matched then Some(best) else None
    end exec_from

The last arm cannot be reached. Everything that does not consume a character is followed by the
closure and never lands in the list, so an instruction arriving here means the closure and this loop
disagree about which those are — a mistake in the engine rather than in the pattern, which is why it
stops the program the way an index past the end of a `Buf` does.

The `here.unwrap() == want` above is the one place a character is tested against a literal, and it
is a comparison of characters rather than of bytes — which is why a two-byte character in the input
is one step here and cannot be half-matched by a pattern holding one of its bytes.

The loop runs **one position past the end** of the input, which is not an off-by-one: `$` is a
zero-width assertion that only holds there, and a pattern ending in `a*` has to be given the chance
to accept after the last character it consumed. That is why the exit is decided at the bottom from
whether there was a character, rather than by a range over the input's length.
